A||N||6||vettepaul||Alignment settings----HELP! ||||1||
Z||000000||vettepaul||12-13-2000||02:46 PM||||I am in the process of having all 4 wheels aligned on our '86 GT.Do any of you guys know the figures.We find it very difficult here in the U.K to get information like this.              Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||12-13-2000||04:24 PM||||          Front<P>    caster                 camber<BR>range 3-7 degrees       -5/16 to 1 5/16 deg.<BR>preferred 5 degrees      1/2 degrees<P>toe-in 1/16 to 1/4 inches<BR>axis   9 3/8 degrees<P>         Rear<BR>   caster                 camber<BR>none             range -1 1/2 to 1/2 degrees<BR>                 preferred      -1 degrees<BR>toe-in   3/32 out 3/32 in<BR>preferred   0||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-13-2000||07:58 PM||||Here's all of it I have from GM doc's. <P>84-87 Fiero, all models.<BR>All #'s in degrees.<BR> <BR>Setting, prefered, tolerance<P>Front<BR>caster, +5, +-2<BR>Max cross caster, 2<BR>camber, +0.5, +-0.8<BR>Max cross camber, 1.6<BR>total toe, 0, +-0.2<BR>steering wheel angle, +-5<P>Rear<BR>camber, -1, +-0.5<BR>toe, 0, +-0.2<BR>thrust angle, 0, +-0.15<P>Cross caster = left cast - right cast<BR>cross camber = left camb - right camb||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||03-17-2001||08:59 PM||||I was talking to a friend at Pontiac and he said the new alignment numbers were to correct tire wear and not handling improvement. The cars were tearing up tires in under 20k miles.<P>Anyone hear this same thing?<P>Terry||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||03-18-2001||01:34 PM||||I hadn't heard anything about it, but that would not suprise me. Small changes can have a big effect there.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||03-18-2001||05:46 PM||||Against all of my better judgement, I installed poly cradle bushings in the rear since the rubber top rears are no longer available from GM. I still have to install the fronts but I want to take the bushings into work and correct the error in the Prothane bushings. They are milled wrong. They won't position correctly in the housings. The outer lip is too thick. Minor detail.<P>However, the result is that the steering wheel is now offset a bit. This means the cradle moved. The old bushings actually looked pretty good for being 15 years old. I checked the inner and outer tie-rods (again) and they are fine.<P>The handling didn't change much if any. I'll hold final judgement for when the fronts are in, but I still think the alignment is wrong.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||06-20-2001||12:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Here's all of it I have from GM doc's. <P>84-87 Fiero, all models.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok, how 'bout the final word on '88 alignment.  Formula, if it matters.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
